n, 19,949 to be exact. And what do I have? 2 of Diamonds, 7 of Spades. *Ugh*, the dreaded 7-2 offsuit. Statistically the worst starting hand in Texas Hold\'em.\n\nGiven that I have 19,900 and the pot is at least 100, effectively, the opponent shoved all-in pre-flop. Let\'s look at this closely because this format is a little strange. It says everyone starts with 20,000 chips each hand but my cumulative win/loss is +19500. So the chips don\'t matter, it is about my cumulative win/loss until the last hand.\n\nNow, considering the options presented – -2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check) – I\'m facing a true all-in situation. "Check" is obviously a non-starter here. That\'s only if no action has been taken. Calling with this garbage hand would be a disaster. I\'m practically guaranteed to lose most of the time.\n\nMy hand is absolute trash. The opponent\'s shove screams strength. Even against a random hand, I\'m a massive underdog, let alone a reasonable range. The pot odds are terrible; I need about 50% equity to break even, and I\'ve got maybe 30% against a random hand. Call is massively -EV (negative expected value).\n\nSince this is a reset format, each hand is separate, but the cumulative is what matters. If I fold, I lose my blind, a very minimal loss. If I call and lose, I\'m down 20,000. Winning is unlikely. Folding is the obvious play here.\n\nTherefore, my decision is clear: I *must* fold. There is no strategic value in calling this all-in with 7-2 offsuit. I\'ll take the small loss and live to fight another hand. I select -1 (Fold). My reasoning is simple: the hand is terrible, the opponent\'s action screams strength, and preserving chips is the only smart play given the circumstances.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: I hold 2 of Diamonds and 7 of Spades (7-2 offsuit), which is statistically the worst starting hand in Texas Hold'em due to the lack of connectivity, low rank, and different suits. The opponent has effectively moved all-in pre-flop. Calling requires risking my entire stack with a hand that has very poor equity (approximately 32% against a random hand and significantly less against a reasonable shoving range). The expected value of calling is highly negative.\n\nReason: With 7-2 offsuit facing an all-in bet, the probability of winning is far too low to justify calling. Folding minimizes the loss to just the blind posted, protecting the cumulative chip lead.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
